<div style="width: 100%; padding: 20px; text-align: center; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Dream Jobs</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> What Are the Jobs of the Future?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Evaluate arguments in opinion pieces. Reading: Analyze the similarities and differences between two texts.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can evaluate the strength of career predictions in an article, identifying key points and supporting arguments (e.g., "I agree with the argument that there is a growing need for data scientists and software engineers due to technological advancements.").
            Can compare perspectives in two texts about future careers by highlighting differences in predictions and evidence (e.g., "One article supports remote work due to flexibility, while the other highlights challenges related to productivity.").
            Can assess arguments in a text about AI's impact on careers (e.g., "I think the article presents a strong case that AI will replace repetitive tasks.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of images showing various future-oriented jobs (e.g., AI specialist, remote work consultant, renewable energy engineer, virtual reality designer). Students work in pairs to quickly list 2-3 vocabulary words from Lesson 1 that describe each job or the skills required. The teacher asks, "What do these jobs have in common regarding the future?" to activate prior knowledge of the theme "What Are the Jobs of the Future?".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to quickly scan a text for the main idea. The teacher points to a short paragraph on the board about a future job and reads it aloud, demonstrating how to identify keywords and phrases related to a prediction. The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary from the previous lesson, such as "automation," "advancement," "adaptability," and "artificial intelligence," focusing on stress in multisyllabic words. The teacher also models reading a short opinion statement, emphasizing how to identify the main argument.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a text is an 'opinion piece,' what kind of information will it mostly contain? Facts or personal views?" (Expected answer: Personal views/arguments).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we talk about 'evaluating arguments,' what are we trying to figure out about them?" (Expected answer: If they are strong, logical, or well-supported).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Dream Jobs".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher provides two short opinion pieces (Text A and Text B), approximately 200-250 words each, about the future of work and "Dream Jobs." Text A might focus on technological advancement creating new opportunities, emphasizing roles like data scientists and software engineers, and the growth of remote work. Text B might focus on challenges like automation replacing repetitive tasks and the need for human adaptability and new skills in creative industries, while also discussing the evolution of existing jobs. Both texts use vocabulary like <i>demand, advancement, automation, remote work, market, career, prediction, technology, opportunities, industries, AI, employment, innovation, workforce, adaptability, evolve, require, replace, develop, innovate.</i> They also feature future simple (e.g., "Many jobs will disappear."), future continuous (e.g., "People will be acquiring new skills."), and modals for possibilities (e.g., "Some jobs may require advanced education.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Comprehending and evaluating main ideas and supporting arguments in written texts from different perspectives on the future of employment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students quickly read both texts (3 minutes) and answer two gist questions: "What is the main prediction of Text A regarding future jobs?" and "What is Text B's primary concern or focus regarding the future workforce?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students complete a True/False exercise based on specific statements about the content of each text (e.g., "Text A argues that remote work will increase significantly in the future. T/F").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match key arguments (e.g., "AI will create new fields," "Human adaptability is crucial") to the text (Text A or Text B) that supports it.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Identifying and evaluating arguments, supporting evidence, and comparing different perspectives. Grammar: Future simple for predictions, Future continuous for ongoing trends, Modals for possibilities. Vocabulary: nouns: <i>jobs, skills, future, demand, advancement, automation, remote work, market, career, prediction, technology, opportunities, industries, AI, employment, innovation, workforce, adaptability, education</i>; verbs: <i>disappear, evolve, work, adapt, acquire, require, advance, change, predict, automate, replace, develop, demand, learn, prepare, innovate, foresee, anticipate, grow</i>; adjectives: <i>future, relevant, technological, automated, remote, in-demand, adaptable, advanced, evolving, innovative</i>. Pronunciation: Stress in multisyllabic words and contrastive sounds /dʒ/ in "change" and /ʒ/ in "vision." Pragmatic: Asking about future career paths.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Argument Identification (Controlled Practice):</b> Students reread each text, individually highlighting sentences that express a main argument or a prediction. They then complete a chart with two columns for each text: "Main Argument/Prediction" and "Key Supporting Idea/Evidence." For example, under Text A: Argument - "Technological advancement will boost new job opportunities," Evidence - "A growing need for data scientists and software engineers."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Perspective Comparison (Semi-Controlled Practice):</b> In pairs, students use a Venn diagram or a comparison table to analyze the similarities and differences in the arguments presented in Text A and Text B. They identify predictions where the texts agree (e.g., both mention the importance of innovation) and where they differ (e.g., one highlights remote work benefits, the other challenges). The teacher circulates, checking for correct identification and appropriate use of vocabulary. During this activity, the teacher listens for and provides feedback on the pronunciation of multisyllabic words like "automation" and "adaptability."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Evaluating Strength of Arguments (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Still in pairs, students discuss which arguments in each text they find most convincing and why, citing specific phrases from the text. They practice pragmatic functions like asking, "What careers do you think will be in demand according to Text A?" and explaining, "I agree with the argument that AI will replace repetitive tasks because the text gives strong examples." They also practice the future simple ("Many new jobs will emerge") and modals for possibility ("Jobs may require constant learning").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">15-17 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Dream Jobs".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format: Group Mini-Debate / Structured Discussion.</b> Students form small groups of 4. Each group chooses one main argument from Text A and one from Text B (e.g., "AI will only create new jobs" vs. "AI will largely replace human jobs"). Within their group, they will engage in a mini-debate or a structured discussion, presenting and defending the arguments from the texts, evaluating their strengths, and comparing the perspectives. They must use evidence from the articles to support their points and express their personal evaluation of the predictions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group is assigned a "side" (to argue for Text A's main point or Text B's main point about a specific issue, e.g., AI's impact, remote work).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students use phrases like, "Text A clearly states that..." or "I think the article presents a strong case that...", "One article supports remote work due to flexibility, while the other highlights challenges related to productivity." They integrate future simple, future continuous ("In ten years, people will be working more remotely"), and modals for possibilities into their arguments.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must also use a minimum of five vocabulary words from the lesson (e.g., <i>innovation, demand, evolve, adaptability, automation</i>) naturally during their discussion.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages students to adapt their tone and language choices based on whether they are addressing a peer or hypothetically a more formal audience, practicing sociolinguistic competence.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on students' ability to evaluate and compare arguments from the texts, and their use of target language. Teacher monitors group discussions, taking notes on individual contributions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly summarizes their discussion, highlighting the key similarities and differences they found between the texts' predictions and their evaluation of the arguments. One or two students from each group present for 1-2 minutes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can evaluate the strength of career predictions in an article, identifying key points and supporting arguments.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can compare perspectives in two texts about future careers by highlighting differences in predictions and evidence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., <i>demand, advancement, automation, adaptability, innovate</i>) accurately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates intelligible pronunciation, including stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "automation," "adaptability").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effectively collaborates and participates in group discussion.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of comparing the arguments in the two articles?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea I learned about the future of jobs from these articles is..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">7-8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did identifying the arguments help you understand the articles better?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What skills do you think you need to improve to better understand different opinions in texts?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., Students were engaged in identifying arguments, the pair comparison was effective for critical thinking). What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Provide more explicit sentence starters for comparing and contrasting, allocate more time for group presentations, focus more on /dʒ/ and /ʒ/ sounds during feedback for specific words like "change" or "vision").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson will focus on Speaking & Interaction. The reading and evaluation of arguments in this lesson will serve as a foundation for students to express their own opinions and discuss future career paths more fluently and confidently in a simulated interview or presentation setting.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>